/*
==============================================================
  DATE AND TIME FORMAT:  yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss
==============================================================
  Example:   2026 - 10 - 28   07 : 18 : 35

  Part      | Format  | Value | Note
  ----------+---------+-------+-----------------------------
  Year      | yyyy    | 2026  | 4 digits
  Month     | MM      | 10    | uppercase M = month
  Day       | dd      | 28    | 2 digits
  Hour      | HH      | 07    | HH = 24h  (hh = 12h)
  Minutes   | mm      | 18    | lowercase m = minute
  Seconds   | ss      | 35    | 2 digits
==============================================================
*/
--MM vs mm is the classic trap: uppercase is month, lowercase is minute. Swap them and yyyy-mm-dd returns “2026-18-28"

/*==============================================================
	 Date standard Representations:

	 yyyy-MM-dd -> International Standard (ISO 8601)   -> SQL Server uses that formart

	 MM-dd-yyyy -> USA Standard

	 dd-MM-yyyy -> European Standard
 ===============================================================*/
